{"qid":"Q769","names":["Grenada"],"title":"Grenada","endonym":{"native":"State of Grenada","lang":"en"},"capsule":"Grenada, the southernmost Windward Island, achieved independence from the United Kingdom on 7 February 1974 under Prime Minister Eric Gairy, but political instability followed quickly: Maurice Bishop's New Jewel Movement seized power in a bloodless coup in 1979, establishing a socialist government aligned with Cuba and the Soviet Union. Bishop was deposed and executed by a hard-line faction of his own party on 19 October 1983, precipitating a U.S.-led military intervention (Operation Urgent Fury) six days later. The intervention restored parliamentary governance, and Grenada subsequently developed a stable democracy; Herbert Blaize and later Keith Mitchell dominated politics through the 1990s and 2000s. The country's economy rests on nutmeg production — Grenada is the world's second-largest nutmeg exporter — alongside cocoa, mace, and tourism.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern sovereign island state with well-defined boundaries; territorial extent is unambiguous.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Grenada","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Grenada"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Grenada","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grenada"}],"britannica":"place/Grenada","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Grenada","rulers":[{"qid":"Q9682","name":"Elizabeth II","start":1974,"end":2022,"image":"Queen Elizabeth II official portrait for 1959 tour (retouched) (cropped) (3-to-4 aspect ratio).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Queen%20Elizabeth%20II%20official%20